In VPC, a subnet is divided into smaller networks, and resources like GKE nodes and databases are assigned with IP ranges using the subnets.
GK will get IP addresses from the subnet using the IP ranges.
So when we create a load balancer service in GKE, it automatically provisions the cloud load balancer. And there are two types of load balancer. One is an internal load balancer, and another one is external load balancer. So internal load balancer works to communicate internally within the service to route the traffic internally. And external load balancer will help to route the traffic from the external users, like incoming traffic is routed to the service and service will route the traffic to the pods. So based on the request, again, pod will route the traffic to the service and service will route the traffic to the load balancer, and load balancer will send the request back to the end user, like external users.
Internal load balancer is used to communicate internally in the VPC between these services, and external load balancer is used to communicate with external users and to pass the incoming traffic to the service, and service will pass that traffic to the pods.
In the architecture, firewall rules will apply in the subnets where the GKE nodes running as a virtual machines to restrict the incoming traffic and outgoing traffic for maintaining the secure communication within the VPC between the resources or services.
Flow from user to port in GKE will happen with the help of external load balancer, like getting the incoming traffic from user and load balancer will route the traffic to the service and service will route the traffic to the pod which is in ready state in the GKE.
The role of VPC in GKE is nothing but to maintain the isolated network to the GKE resources and secure the incoming and outgoing traffic between the resources available in GKE for maintaining secure and consistent communication.
